[
  {
    "name": "listCapabilities",
    "description": "Lists what this key may do: the collections and globals it can read or write, whether a collection can also be created in, their draft behaviour and id type, the configured locales, the limits in force and the custom tools available. Call it first to orient; nothing here changes with the content model.\n\nA global is a singleton: it has no id, is not listed by findDocuments and cannot be created. Address one with the \"global\" argument where a collection document would take \"collection\" and \"id\".",
    "inputSchema": {
      "$schema": "http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#",
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {},
      "additionalProperties": false
    },
    "annotations": {
      "readOnlyHint": true,
      "openWorldHint": false
    },
    "execution": {
      "taskSupport": "forbidden"
    }
  },
  {
    "name": "describeSchema",
    "description": "Describes the shape of a document, one node at a time.\n\nPass exactly one of \"collection\" and \"global\". A global is a singleton: it has no id, is not listed by findDocuments and cannot be created.\n\nCall it with no \"paths\" to get the own fields of the collection or global. Every \"blocks\" field stops there and lists the block slugs it accepts instead of nesting them; each node's \"next\" lists the ready-to-use paths for those blocks, so pass any entry of \"next\" as a \"paths\" element to descend, e.g. \"/layout/sections/sectionWrapper\" and then \"/layout/sections/sectionWrapper/modules/hero\". A block is described as it exists at that position, because the same block can accept different children elsewhere.\n\nA \"richText\" field stops there too. It lists the Lexical node types it accepts in \"nodes\", and \"next\" carries a path for every node type that holds fields of its own: \"/content/link\" for a link node, \"/content/block/callout\" and \"/content/inlineBlock/badge\" for the block nodes. Descend to get the real field list instead of guessing what a node carries. Upload nodes are not addressable, because their fields depend on the collection the node points at.\n\nWrite each Lexical node the way Lexical serializes it, with every property its type carries rather than a trimmed subset, and with the value Lexical would have written there. Those requirements are stated rather than left to be discovered: the response carries one final \"nodeProperties\" entry keyed by node type, naming each property and what belongs there in the same words a refused write uses, so a node can be built from this response alone. A field's own \"nodes\" says which of those types it accepts. The root takes exactly \"children\", \"direction\", \"format\", \"indent\", \"type\" and \"version\" and refuses anything else. The admin editor rehydrates nodes through their classes, so a list item whose \"indent\" is missing, null or a string is stored and then throws on open, and a heading whose \"tag\" is a number is stored untagged. A write naming a property means exactly that. A state whose root holds no children is refused however it is written, because Lexical reads it as empty and throws; clear a field with null instead.\n\nA rich text field's value is addressable too, so an edit does not have to rewrite the whole state: \"/content/root/children/0\" is the first top-level node, \"/content/root/children/0/children/1\" a node inside it, \"/content/root/children/0/tag\" one property of a node, and \"/content/root/children/0/fields/url\" a field of a node, described at the \"next\" path for that node type. Append a node with \"/-\". Which node sits at an index is only knowable from what is stored, so read it first: getDocument with \"outline\" answers with the pointer, type, \"version\" and a text excerpt for every node, which is far cheaper than reading the whole state.\n\nPaths here use the same JSON Pointer syntax as getDocument and patchDocument, and are already resolved through anything that does not nest in the stored document. The difference is only what stands in an element position: a path names an array element \"*\" and a block by its slug, where a pointer into a document carries a 0-based index. So \"/items/*/title\" is written at \"/items/0/title\", and \"/layout/sections/hero\" at \"/layout/sections/0\". Inside a rich text field that substitution does not apply: a path there names the node type, and a block node its slug, where a pointer enters the stored state at \"root\" and walks \"children\" by an index counted over every child at that level, not over the blocks among them, with the node's own fields under \"fields\". So \"/content/block/callout/tone\" is written at \"/content/root/children/7/fields/tone\".\n\nFields Payload maintains (id, _status, createdAt, updatedAt) are never listed and cannot be written. Fields marked readOnly are listed but refused on write.",
    "inputSchema": {
      "$schema": "http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#",
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
        "collection": {
          "description": "Collection to describe.",
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [
            "pages",
            "posts",
            "tags",
            "notes",
            "snippets",
            "media"
          ]
        },
        "global": {
          "description": "Global to describe.",
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [
            "site-settings",
            "banner"
          ]
        },
        "paths": {
          "description": "Schema paths to describe, e.g. \"/layout/sections/sectionWrapper\". Omit for the root.",
          "maxItems": 400,
          "type": "array",
          "items": {
            "type": "string"
          }
        },
        "expand": {
          "description": "Return every node reachable from the root in one response. Ignores paths.",
          "type": "boolean"
        }
      },
      "additionalProperties": false
    },
    "annotations": {
      "readOnlyHint": true,
      "openWorldHint": false
    },
    "execution": {
      "taskSupport": "forbidden"
    }
  },
  {
    "name": "findDocuments",
    "description": "Finds documents in a collection. \"where\" is a Payload query object, e.g. {\"title\":{\"contains\":\"home\"}} or {\"and\":[...]}; \"select\" picks fields, e.g. {\"title\":true}. Drafts are included by default so unpublished work is visible. Keep depth at 0 unless populated relationships are needed; ids are enough for writes.",
    "inputSchema": {
      "$schema": "http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#",
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
        "collection": {
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [
            "pages",
            "posts",
            "tags",
            "notes",
            "snippets",
            "media"
          ],
          "description": "Collection to search."
        },
        "where": {
          "description": "Payload where query.",
          "type": "object",
          "propertyNames": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "additionalProperties": {}
        },
        "sort": {
          "description": "Sort field, prefix with \"-\" for descending.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "limit": {
          "description": "Documents per page. Default 10, at most 25.",
          "type": "integer",
          "minimum": 1,
          "maximum": 25
        },
        "page": {
          "description": "Page number, from 1.",
          "type": "integer",
          "minimum": 1,
          "maximum": 9007199254740991
        },
        "depth": {
          "description": "Relationship population depth. Default 0, at most 1.",
          "type": "integer",
          "minimum": 0,
          "maximum": 1
        },
        "select": {
          "description": "Fields to return, e.g. {\"title\":true}.",
          "type": "object",
          "propertyNames": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "additionalProperties": {}
        },
        "locale": {
          "description": "Locale to read. Defaults to the default locale.",
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [
            "en",
            "de"
          ]
        },
        "draft": {
          "description": "Include the latest drafts. Default true.",
          "type": "boolean"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "collection"
      ],
      "additionalProperties": false
    },
    "annotations": {
      "readOnlyHint": true,
      "openWorldHint": false
    },
    "execution": {
      "taskSupport": "forbidden"
    }
  },
  {
    "name": "getDocument",
    "description": "Reads one document, or one subtree of it when \"path\" is given as a JSON pointer such as \"/layout/sections/2\". Returns the latest draft by default. Read before patching: the response carries \"updatedAt\" for expectedUpdatedAt and the indices pointers need.\n\nPass exactly one of \"collection\" and \"global\". \"id\" is required with \"collection\" and must be omitted with \"global\", because a global is a singleton.\n\nSet \"outline\" on a rich text \"path\" to get a compact positional listing of its nodes instead of the raw editor state.\n\nOn an entity that exposes versions, \"versionId\" (from findVersions) reads that version instead. \"diffFrom\" returns the RFC 6902 operations turning a version, or \"published\" (the newest version with published status, regardless of locale), into the document read, in the pointer syntax patchDocument takes and limited to \"path\" when given. To revert, pass the old version as \"versionId\" and the latest one from findVersions as \"diffFrom\", then apply the patch with patchDocument.",
    "inputSchema": {
      "$schema": "http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#",
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
        "collection": {
          "description": "Collection holding the document.",
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [
            "pages",
            "posts",
            "tags",
            "notes",
            "snippets",
            "media"
          ]
        },
        "global": {
          "description": "Global to read.",
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [
            "site-settings",
            "banner"
          ]
        },
        "id": {
          "description": "Document id. Required with \"collection\"; must be omitted with \"global\".",
          "type": [
            "string",
            "number"
          ]
        },
        "path": {
          "description": "JSON pointer to return only a subtree, e.g. \"/layout/sections/0\".",
          "type": "string",
          "pattern": "^(\\/([^~/]|~[01])*)*$"
        },
        "depth": {
          "description": "Relationship population depth. Default 0, at most 1.",
          "type": "integer",
          "minimum": 0,
          "maximum": 1
        },
        "locale": {
          "description": "Locale to read. Defaults to the default locale.",
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [
            "en",
            "de"
          ]
        },
        "draft": {
          "description": "Return the latest draft. Default true.",
          "type": "boolean"
        },
        "outline": {
          "description": "For a rich text field, return a compact positional outline instead of the editor state. Requires \"path\".",
          "type": "boolean"
        },
        "versionId": {
          "description": "Version to read instead of the document, from findVersions. Only for entities that expose versions; not with \"draft\".",
          "type": [
            "string",
            "number"
          ]
        },
        "diffFrom": {
          "description": "Version id, or \"published\" for the newest published version in any locale, to diff from. Returns {from, to, patch} instead of the document. Only for entities that expose versions.",
          "type": [
            "string",
            "number"
          ]
        }
      },
      "additionalProperties": false
    },
    "annotations": {
      "readOnlyHint": true,
      "openWorldHint": false
    },
    "execution": {
      "taskSupport": "forbidden"
    }
  },
  {
    "name": "findVersions",
    "description": "Lists the version history of one document or global, newest first. Returns metadata only; read a version's body with getDocument and \"versionId\", or what changed with \"diffFrom\".\n\nPass exactly one of \"collection\" and \"global\". \"id\" is required with \"collection\" and must be omitted with \"global\". Autosave versions are listed too, marked \"autosave\".",
    "inputSchema": {
      "$schema": "http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#",
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
        "collection": {
          "description": "Collection holding the document.",
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [
            "pages",
            "snippets"
          ]
        },
        "global": {
          "description": "Global whose history to list.",
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [
            "site-settings"
          ]
        },
        "id": {
          "description": "Document id. Required with \"collection\"; must be omitted with \"global\".",
          "type": [
            "string",
            "number"
          ]
        },
        "limit": {
          "description": "Versions per page. Default 10, at most 25.",
          "type": "integer",
          "minimum": 1,
          "maximum": 25
        },
        "page": {
          "description": "Page number, from 1.",
          "type": "integer",
          "minimum": 1,
          "maximum": 9007199254740991
        },
        "status": {
          "description": "Only versions with this status.",
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [
            "published",
            "draft"
          ]
        },
        "locale": {
          "description": "Locale whose status to report. Defaults to the default locale.",
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [
            "en",
            "de"
          ]
        }
      },
      "additionalProperties": false
    },
    "annotations": {
      "readOnlyHint": true,
      "openWorldHint": false
    },
    "execution": {
      "taskSupport": "forbidden"
    }
  }
]
